 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server holds an open connection per connected browser and pushes each committed revision as it lands, and browsers reconnect and catch up on missed revisions after a dropped connection. This realizes updates in well under a second without depending on a realtime vendor, in exchange for owning connection capacity and reconnect behavior.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around sockets. The change store keeps replayable revisions and a reliable publication path so a committed edit survives the gap between commit and push. The delivery node manages socket sessions and publishes committed revisions to them. The browser-sync node owns reconnect and catch-up, replaying missed revisions to recover the latest board.
 
