 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Committed changes delivered over self-hosted WebSockets.** The server holds a connection per connected browser and pushes each committed revision as it lands, with reconnect and catch-up after a drop. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it adds seconds of lag and repeated reads without removing operational surface. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it reaches low latency sooner but trades recurring fees and provider coupling for connection operations the team can own. Self-hosting reaches updates in under a second and keeps the board free of a realtime vendor.
